{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "_comment": [
    "Organisations an Indian scam message impersonates. Consumed by two places:",
    "  1. Brand-claim extraction (design.md §4.1) matches `aliases` against normalized text.",
    "  2. Typosquat detection (design.md §3.2) compares candidate hosts against `domains`.",
    "",
    "ALIAS PRECISION IS THE WHOLE GAME. A brand claim gates the URL analyzer's force-verdict",
    "rule (§3.5), so an over-broad alias turns a legitimate message into a forced SCAM verdict.",
    "Never add a bare common noun: not `bank`, not `pay`, not `card`, not `post`. Every alias",
    "here should be a string that a message has essentially no innocent reason to contain",
    "unless it is talking about this organisation.",
    "",
    "`dlt_headers` are the body of the TRAI DLT sender ID, i.e. the part after the two-letter",
    "operator prefix: `VM-SBIINB` has header `SBIINB`. The registry is not public, so this list",
    "is best-effort from publicly observed commercial SMS and is deliberately incomplete.",
    "That is safe by construction: DLT_HEADER_MISMATCH (design.md §4.2) fires only when a",
    "header is known AND maps to a different brand. An unknown header produces no evidence.",
    "",
    "A header may legitimately belong to more than one brand here — GOOGLE covers both Google",
    "and Google Pay, AMAZON covers both Amazon and Amazon Pay. The loader must therefore build",
    "header -> SET of brand ids, and the mismatch rule must fire only when that set is disjoint",
    "from the set of brands the message claims. A one-to-one map would raise DLT_HEADER_MISMATCH",
    "on genuine Google Pay and Amazon Pay messages, which is exactly the class of false positive",
    "the hard-negative work exists to prevent."
  ],

